WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Or the strange biology of the Ampropenguin.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myths and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The curiosities I find most fascinating of all.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Some of our most familiar animals puzzled scientific minds for a surprisingly long time.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.000
The mysterious comings and goings of barn swallows led to some far-fetched ideas.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
While the life cycle of the painted lady butterfly took centuries to unravel.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Swallows have successfully nested and raised their young in this barn for several years.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.000
These chicks will soon leave the nest and make their first exploratory flights around the farm.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
But in a few weeks time they will suddenly vanish.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Where do they go to?

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
In the past that gave rise to some extraordinary speculations.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
In fact in the 18th century it became a very long-running debate headed by some well-known church figures.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.000
And swallows are not the only birds that appear and disappear with the changing seasons.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
For centuries people speculated about where such birds go.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
One explanation was that some birds changed into others by growing different adult plumage.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Perhaps the red start turned into a robin.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Or the garden warbler into a black cap.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Since these species were seldom present at the same time the explanation seemed entirely plausible.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.000
The barnacle goose was another mystery.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Each winter huge noisy flocks of them appear on European shores apparently from out of nowhere.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000
No one had ever seen them build a nest or raise young.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:53.000
The barnacle goose gave rise to some extraordinary folklore as this medieval illustration shows.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.000
It was thought that the geese grew on underwater trees.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Starting life as small marine creatures called goose barnacles.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Goose barnacles do of course exist.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.000
They're small shelled marine organisms with what looks like the head.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Which is in fact enclosed by a shell.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Attached by a stalk which is thought to resemble the neck of a bird to a bit of wood or a rock.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:29.000
The confusion about the nature of the barnacle goose was put to good use by some.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Since it was unclear whether it was a bird, a fish or some other creature.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.000
You could surely be allowed to eat it on days when meat was forbidden by the church.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:47.000
But the most commonly held belief was that birds disappear in winter because they hibernated.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Swallows and their close relatives the swifts and martins were thought to do so in mud at the bottom of ponds and rivers.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:02.000
And it's easy to see how this idea originated because the birds spend much of their time near water.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Skimming low over the surface hunting for insects or taking a drink.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
It wasn't until the middle ages that another theory was proposed that some birds may migrate.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.000
And one of its strongest proponents was an influential religious leader.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Frederick the second of Hohenstaufen was a powerful Holy Roman Emperor and known for his unorthodox views.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:39.000
He ignored the philosophy of the church and based his knowledge of natural history on direct observation rather than what was ordained.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Frederick was also a keen falconer and he wrote this book The Art of Falconry.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.000
And in it surprisingly there are entire chapters on the migration of birds.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:01.000
His confidence came from the fact that unlike his contemporaries and those before him he had actually observed birds in the field for himself.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:07.000
He had no doubt about the migration and so little patience for the myths surrounding the barnacle goose.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:16.000
He considered the story to be quite ridiculous and argued that the birds simply breed in distant lands.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:28.000
His views started a debate that split people into two camps. Those believing in the old hibernation theory and those who supported the idea that birds migrate.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:37.000
This was the start of a new era which was to sweep away myths and focus instead on facts and careful observation.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:44.000
And across Europe the evidence for bird migration started to accumulate.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:57.000
In Germany a 12th century monk is said to have taken a swallow from its nest and attached a parchment note to its leg that read, oh swallow where do you live in winter?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:06.000
The following spring the bird returned with a note saying, in Asia in the home of Petrus that is Israel.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:14.000
The story may not have been true but it certainly gave the right hint.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:25.000
In the early 16th century a bishop from Sweden called Olaus Magnus reignited the debate about swallows with this picture.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:32.000
He claimed that in winter fishermen often drew up swallows in their nets hanging together in a mass.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:48.000
This astonishing assertion provided ample fuel for the anti-migration lobby and unlikely as it was the view that swallows spent their winters underwater became increasingly entrenched.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:07:01.000
By the 18th century the debate about migration versus hibernation had come to a head and across the continent opinions were divided.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:06.000
But new evidence was about to come from an unusual source.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:13.000
Edward Jenner was an English country doctor who also had a deep interest in natural history.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:21.000
He noted that although swallows often splash in water as they skim across it they never immerse themselves.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:28.000
Were they to do so he suggested their wings would become so wet that they would be unable to fly.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.000
To test his idea Jenner reportedly held a swift underwater for two minutes.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:38.000
Not surprisingly it died.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.000
Jenner went on to devise another experiment to discover where the birds go.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:49.000
He took 12 swifts from their nests and marked them by taking off two of their claws.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:55.000
The following year some of the birds he had marked were caught again in exactly the same spot.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Although Jenner could not discover where his swifts had been over the winter he was the first to show that they returned to use the same breeding sites in the following years.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.000
And we now know that this is true for swallows as well.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:18.000
About the same time across the channel a German bird enthusiast had come up with a similar idea.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:28.000
Johann Frisch caught several birds near his house and attached to their legs wooden threads like this which he had dipped in red watercolour.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:35.000
He predicted that if swallows really did spend the winter at the bottom of lakes the red colour would be washed off.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:41.000
The following spring Frisch's swallows returned and the threads were unchanged.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.000
It was a very simple but very effective experiment.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:54.000
Evidence against the hibernation theory continued to mount and eventually a new technique put the final nail in its coffin.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.000
Systematic bird ringing.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:03.000
This bird has just been fitted with its own individual marker.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.000
A small metal ring on its leg with a unique code of numbers.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:17.000
It's part of a national scheme that's been running for over a hundred years and provides scientists with invaluable data on bird movements.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Early in the 20th century the study of migration really took off.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:27.000
Birds were recovered on their breeding and wintering grounds and often en route too.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:37.000
600 years after Frederick von Hohenstaufen had first started the debate real evidence was beginning to accumulate.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:45.000
In the summer of 1911 a metal ring just like this one was clipped onto the leg of a young swallow in Staffordshire.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.000
The number on the ring was B830.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.000
18 months later the same bird was caught by a farmer in South Africa.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:03.000
Here at last was the indisputable proof that swallows migrate and spend the winter thousands of miles away.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:09.000
Off you go. There we are.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Today of course we know that the swallow's migration is one of the most impressive in all the animal kingdom.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.000
It takes it across the largest desert in the world, the Sahara.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:28.000
It's a grueling and dangerous journey and many die on the way from exhaustion or starvation.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:42.000
They travel for nearly four months covering nearly 6,000 miles and eventually reach southern Africa.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:48.000
And bird ringing also helped to dispel the myth of the barnacle goose.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:55.000
In the 1960s a Norwegian expedition ringed geese nesting on the Arctic island of Spitsbergen.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:03.000
That autumn some of the same birds were sighted on the west coast of Scotland over a thousand miles away.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:11.000
Frederik von Hohenstaufen had been proved to be absolutely correct.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:17.000
It took centuries to discover the truth behind the swallow's seasonal movements.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:26.000
But in their time they baffled the minds of many great naturalists and started one of the longest running of all scientific debates.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:37.000
But in the end the true story proved to be even more extraordinary than the fantastic myths that were invented to explain it.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:47.000
Coming up we unravel the life of another creature whose sudden appearance and disappearance puzzled us, the painted lady butterfly.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:59.000
Just like the swallow the painted lady seems to appear magically out of nowhere and that started some extraordinary ideas and controversies.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:10.000
The painted lady is one of our largest butterflies and a familiar summer visitor to our gardens and yet its appearance and disappearance each year has puzzled us for centuries.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:18.000
It's only now that we're beginning to understand this extraordinary life cycle and discover where it vanishes each year.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:29.000
Early naturalists were confused by the sudden appearance of painted ladies each spring because they were unaware of the connection between butterflies and caterpillars.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:40.000
For a very long time it was widely believed that butterflies arise from rotting material by what was called spontaneous generation.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:50.000
In the 1830s a German scientist named Renaud was arrested for heresy for claiming that he could change caterpillars into butterflies.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:13:05.000
Arresting someone for something now known to be common knowledge may seem rather extreme but at the time many still believed that caterpillars and butterflies were completely different creatures created by the hand of God.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:14.000
Needless to say people had been well aware of the existence of both butterflies and caterpillars since the earliest times.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:26.000
But the thought that any two were related let alone the same species seemed impossible and it's easy to see why.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:37.000
Not only do caterpillars and butterflies look like very different types of animals but the colors and patterns of a caterpillar don't match up with those of its adult form.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:46.000
The only way to know which larvae and which butterfly go together is to keep caterpillars and watch them turn into butterflies.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:52.000
But it wasn't until the 17th century that anyone left a record of doing that.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.000
One of the first was a remarkable woman named Maria Sibylla Merian.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:07.000
Merian was born in Germany at a time when women still had little formal education and no role in the scientific world.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:13.000
But she was an accomplished artist and painted plants and insects she saw around her.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:21.000
To do that she kept caterpillars, fed them on leaves and watched them turn into butterflies.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:29.000
Merian produced hundreds of beautiful paintings of butterflies in their stages of development along with the plants on which they feed.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:37.000
Her drawings are so exquisite and detailed that they still rank among the best in the world.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:42.000
Among the things she observed with great care were things like this.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:47.000
A curious yet strangely beautiful object.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:56.000
It's a chrysalis, the intermediate stage between a caterpillar and a butterfly.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:03.000
She was one of the first to record the remarkable change that takes place in the chrysalis.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:11.000
It's one of nature's most extraordinary transformations.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:20.000
At the age of 52 she sailed from Europe to South America on a two-year expedition to study insects in the tropical jungles of Suriname.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:26.000
It was an exceptional journey for any naturalist at the time and particularly for a woman.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:32.000
When she returned she produced this beautiful book.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:40.000
It turned out to be popular because it was one of the few to be published not in the scientific language of Latin but in Dutch.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:45.000
Because of this her work was largely dismissed by scientists of the time.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:54.000
But Merian was one of the first naturalists to correctly connect the caterpillar with its pupa and the adult form.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:01.000
Today Merian's book is widely recognized as a pioneering work of scientific observation.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:08.000
And it put an end to the idea of spontaneous generation.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:18.000
Around the same time further evidence for the connection between butterflies and caterpillars came from a different source.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:30.000
In 1669 a Dutch scientist by the name of Jan Swammerdam published the results of experiments which would finally prove that the caterpillar and butterfly are one and the same animal.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:38.000
Swammerdam was a master of the miniature and dissected the caterpillars and pupae of butterflies and moths under a microscope.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:49.000
With a steady hand and endless patience he carefully cut into the layers of skin with tiny scissors and what he discovered was truly astonishing.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:54.000
He found some of the body parts of a butterfly.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:08.000
The structures were fragile and not complete but Swammerdam had proved that caterpillar and butterfly are indeed one and the same animal.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:12.000
We now know that without the caterpillar there can be no butterfly.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:16.000
Yet for a very long time the painted lady seemed to be an exception.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:23.000
Every spring the adult butterflies would appear across Britain without any sightings of their caterpillars.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:29.000
While some butterflies hibernate in Britain there was no sign of painted ladies doing so.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:35.000
Some speculated that they flew to warmer climates as birds do.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:39.000
But how could a tiny insect cross the English Channel?

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:49.000
In the 20th century swarms of butterflies moving across Europe finally provided evidence that painted ladies do indeed cross the sea.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:54.000
And they were found to fly all the way from North Africa to Britain.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:18:00.000
But there were almost no records of painted ladies making the reverse trip south.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:08.000
So for years it was thought that Britain must be a dead end for the most northerless draculas.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:14.000
And then in 2009 the public was asked to help solve the mystery.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:22.000
Among 12,000 sightings there were reports of painted ladies flying out to sea in the autumn.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:32.000
And a radar station detected them flying south at heights of 500 metres, way beyond the sight of human eyes.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:39.000
We now know that the painted ladies migration is a round trip of over 7,000 miles.

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:42.000
But it's not made by any one individual.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:48.000
Each only flies part of the way, passing on the migratory baton to the next generation.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:55.000
It's like a relay race with up to six generations of butterflies involved.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:02.000
The painted ladies epic journey from one continent to the next would be a truly astonishing feat for any animal.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:06.000
But for a tiny creature like this it seems really extraordinary.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:11.000
How does it battle the wind and the weather and navigate across vast bodies of water?

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:18.000
With no single individual ever undertaking the whole migration, how do they find the way?

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:25.000
It seems that painted ladies are pre-programmed to either fly north or south.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:34.000
And this is determined whilst they're still caterpillars, possibly by temperature and day length and also by the plants they feed on.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:39.000
But how does this information get passed on from caterpillar to butterfly?

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:43.000
The answer may be hidden within the chrysalis.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:49.000
Recently, CT scanners have allowed us to look inside a pupa.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:56.000
They reveal that some organs remain intact during the transformation.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:07.000
A one-day-old pupa clearly shows the gut and breathing tubes, which only change slightly as the chrysalis develops.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:15.000
Could it be that the brain or nerves also remain intact and that memories are passed on?

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:20.000
Recent experiments in the lab appear to support this idea.

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:28.000
Scientists taught caterpillars to avoid specific smells by linking them with an unpleasant reaction.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:35.000
Later on, as adults, the same individuals remembered these smells and chose to keep away from them.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:45.000
If the experiences of a caterpillar can be carried over to the adult, then maybe cues for migration can also be passed on.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:51.000
Although we've unraveled much of the painted ladies' life cycle, many questions remain.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:54.000
How far does each individual travel?

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:58.000
And do offspring follow similar routes to their ancestors?

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:07.000
One day, we may know the answers, but for now, they remain some of the unsolved mysteries of nature.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:15.000
The arrival each spring of our painted lady butterflies and our swallows never ceases to delight us.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:28.000
But now we also understand the extraordinary journeys they undertake when they disappear again at the end of summer.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:50.000
Thank you for watching!
